Hi [~accountid:712020:3d2dfcb9-5b90-4b13-a4e2-9b7530e3d83f], thank you for your patience 🙏 

I have reviewed both questions, please see below:

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
On disclosing the driver’s licensing authority to the rider:
{panel}

We should not disclose the driver’s licensing authority to the rider. The name of the licensing authority becomes +personal data+ once it sits in the driver’s profile as part of their regulatory record. Although “Manchester”, “Sefton” or similar look like administrative labels, in context they describe something specific about that driver and contribute to identifying them. This type of information is not included in the data we share with riders.

Of note, not disclosing this information should not be conceived as preventing the rider from making a complaint, though they may feel this way. They can raise it with Bolt, report it to the police, or contact the licensing authority where the journey took place. My understanding is that in the UK, should the wrong council be approached on this kind of matter, the authority will redirect the passenger to the correct council. 

Long story short: this data should not be provided as it is personal data about the driver. 

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
On disclosing disciplinary information taken against the driver to the rider:
{panel}

In general, I would always advise following this page (co-authored by Privacy): [https://taxify.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/PR/pages/6558056971/PR+-+Legal+crisis+communication|https://taxify.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/PR/pages/6558056971/PR+-+Legal+crisis+communication|smart-link] as it includes an overview of what can be said and how, in terms of actions taken against a driver. In the same logic as above, any action taken against the driver amounts to personal data about them, which the rider is not entitled to. 

As the specific event at hand is not quite a crisis, and as far as I understand no specific actions were taken against the driver, I would advise not elaborating on this topic beyond what has already been said. CS should have KB guidance on this topic.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
Suggested answer:
{panel}

I’ve quickly drafted this answer based on one of our templates - feel free to edit it as you deem fit in the case at hand:

{quote}Dear [NAME],

After carefully reviewing your request for the name of the licensing authority that issued the driver’s Private Hire license and details of any disciplinary actions taken in relation to the driver, we must inform you that we cannot provide this information. The information you requested concerns the driver and does not form part of the personal data that Bolt processes about you. Disclosing it would therefore involve sharing another individual’s personal data to a third party, which we are not permitted to do under Article 15(4) UK GDPR.

Please note that this does not affect your ability to raise complaints. As already communicated, we have reviewed your report in line with our internal processes and taken the appropriate steps. In addition, should you choose to contact the licensing authority responsible for the area where the trip took place, and the matter falls under another authority’s remit, they should redirect your complaint accordingly.

Yours sincerely,
Bolt{quote}

