Hello [~accountid:712020:793bd952-a372-4264-98c5-05f34ecedce8]. Please find below our responses:

_1- Based on this, my understanding is that Bolt would not be directly liable before the contracting authority. Could you please confirm explicitly whether this interpretation is correct?_

{color:#0747a6}According to the Spanish Public Procurement Law, there would be no direct liability before the contracting authority, for the execution of the contract as a subcontractor, {color}{color:#0747a6}*but this is based on the assumption that a subcontractor enters this positions meets all the technical requirements of the tender, as attested by the technical capacity declaration*{color}{color:#0747a6}.{color}

_2 - To understand the risk level:_

* _What specific civil liabilities or penalties could Bolt realistically face?_
* _Are we talking about administrative fines, damages claimed by MZ, or something else?_
* _What would be the financial magnitude we should assume? (e.g., thousands, hundreds of thousands, proportional to contract size, etc.)_
* _What is the probability of such a liability being triggered?_

{color:#0747a6}However, in this case, it is already known that Bolt does not meets such requirements, which the Legal Team believes could open the door for civil liability, both before the contracting authority and MZ (emerging from potencial damages for failure to provide the services stipulated in the tender, that we signed declarations attesting we were in conditions of securing). The amounts cannot precisely be known at the moment, but can be, at least, equivalent to the value of the contract.{color}

{color:#0747a6}The Legal Team cannot calculate the probability of such liability being triggered, considering we have no record of similar previous situations.{color}

_3 - If these requirements (driver control, technical quality, availability, etc.) are structurally present in almost all tenders, does this mean that Bolt will never be in a position to be fully compliant from a strict legal perspective. If the answer is yes, does it follow that any B2G activity will always entail an “assessed risk” scenario, where we knowingly operate in a model that is not 100% aligned with the wording of the tender but is accepted as a calculated business risk?_

_In other words: Are we essentially saying that the situation we face with this tender (MZ) is not an exception, but the “new normal” for any B2G opportunity involving public transportation tenders in Spain?_
_If so, it is important for us to have a clear, principled position on how Bolt wants to approach these tenders going forward (e.g. “never participate where these conditions exist” vs. “participate with a defined risk framework and internal guidelines”)._

{color:#0747a6}Deciding the “{color}{color:#0747a6}_principled position on how Bolt wants to approach these tenders going forward_{color}{color:#0747a6}” is not within the scope the Local Legal Team to decide. That requires a centralized decision to address this issue, on what is the strategy going forward in B2G for Spain, similar to what was done in other countries.{color}

_4 - Can Bolt negotiate a contractual safeguard with MZ that limits or excludes our liability in case the tender is terminated by the contracting authority?_

* _What form could such a safeguard take? (e.g. limitation of liability clause, indemnification, no-fault termination, etc.)_
* _Would such an agreement materially reduce Bolt’s exposure?_

{color:#0747a6}Our preferred approach, as recommended below is that Bolt enters into a contract with MZ, in Bolt’s standard terms, only providing platform access. Without any direct involvement in the tender.{color}

_5 - we urgently need: A clear articulation of the real risks to Bolt; A quantified estimate of potential financial exposure; Confirmation on whether contractual protections with MZ could sufficiently mitigate these risks._

{color:#0747a6}As stated above and in the previous answer below, Bolt is exposed in this set-up to liability towards MZ, as Bolt would commit to tender criteria that is not aligned with the Bolt’s Business mode, which amounts cannot be certainly determined but could amount to the value of the awarded contract.{color}

