h3. Copying for visibility - clarifications were requested by [~accountid:712020:0b391d02-6c8a-4494-99fd-3d2ff0d7ef37] in Slack re/ the following clauses in Agora’s [data processing contract|https://docs.google.com/document/d/1eJiUQsO0sdCPZReAG9ijP2pEgzn00mlF/edit]:

{quote} [@kostiantyn.ponomarov|https://bolt.enterprise.slack.com/team/U05T21T3JMA] could you confirm that if Bolt is a Procesor that Bolt *shall be fully* liable towards the Entrusting Party for failure to comply with the data protection obligations of sub-processors” results from the provisions of the GDPR, specifically from Article 28, paragraph 4 of the GDPR.{quote}

*Response:* The *principle* of liability for sub-processors _does_ come from GDPR. But *the way it is drafted* can absolutely be improved to: avoid Bolt taking on liability even where Bolt clearly isn’t responsible. I would suggest the following amendment:

{quote}*_4.6_*_. The Processor shall remain liable towards the Entrusting Party for the performance by sub-processors of their data protection obligations arising from the Agreement and applicable data protection law, in accordance with Article 28(4) GDPR. The Processor shall not be liable to the extent it proves, in line with Article 82(3) GDPR, that it is not in any way responsible for the event giving rise to the damage, in particular where the damage results from:_
_(i) the Entrusting Party’s unlawful or non-compliant instructions, or_
_(ii) acts or omissions of a sub-processor that are not attributable to the Processor’s failure to comply with its obligations under this Agreement or under applicable data protection law._{quote}

----

{quote}*19. Pkt 8.6. i 8.7.* -> [@kostiantyn.ponomarov|https://bolt.enterprise.slack.com/team/U05T21T3JMA] tell me whether these two points (the two crossed out points) extend Bolt's liability under the GDPR, in particular in terms of Article 28 of the GDPR{quote}

*Response:* Both clauses *expand Bolt’s liability beyond GDPR requirements*, especially:

* Clause 8.6 → expanding categories of claimants + suggesting strict liability.
* Clause 8.7 → forcing full indemnification of fines/compensation, which GDPR does *not* require and may result in unlimited financial exposure for Bolt.

I would suggest the following amendments:

